  "dimensions": [
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c7b8921b",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: the_first_rule_of_deaf_club; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a woman using American Sign Language (ASL) while a man gestures with his hands, with text overlaying the scene. The top text reads \"the first rule of deaf club..\" and the bottom text reads \"nyou nu not nalk amout nef-glub\" (a misspelling of \"you do not talk about deaf club\"). This is a play on the well-known \"Rule of the Club\" trope, where members are forbidden from discussing the club's existence or rules. The meme's intent is to humorously suggest that deaf people are so secretive or exclusive that they don't discuss their own community. While the joke is based on a common trope, it can be considered harmful because it reinforces a stereotype that deaf people are secretive or isolated, which is not accurate. It also potentially trivializes the experience of being deaf by reducing it to a joke about secrecy. The meme does not contain direct abusive, cyberbullying, or hateful content, but it does contain harmful stereotypes that can be offensive to the deaf community.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_336dcc57"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
